.TH SAVECORE 8 "November 13, 2026"
.SH NAME
savecore \- save a crash dump from the SD card
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /sbin/savecore
[
.B \-v
] [
.B \-f
.I device
]
.I directory
.br
.B /sbin/savecore
.B \-c
[
.B \-v
] [
.B \-f
.I device
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
When a PicoCalc kernel panics, or
.B "reboot \-d"
asks for a dump, the kernel writes the panic message, its data and bss
with the SwapRAM pool, and both u areas to the first partition of the SD
card whose MBR type is
.BR 0xb9 ,
provided that no mounted file system overlaps it.
The dump format is
.IR sys/arch/rp2040/include/dump.h .
Only the PICOCALC and PICOCALC_PSRAM kernels running on a PicoCalc write a
dump; every other configuration has no dump device.
.PP
.B savecore
finds that partition through
.I /dev/rsd0
(the device nodes
.I /dev/rsd0c
and
.I /dev/rsd0d
are made with
.IR mknod (8)
when the dump partition is the third or fourth entry),
reads its header and, if a dump is present, checks the header, the
segment table and every segment checksum while it copies the dump to
.IR directory /dump. N
and writes
.IR directory /info. N ,
a text file naming the panic message, the CRC32 of the kernel image that
wrote the dump, the running kernel's image CRC32, the time and the
segment table.
The image CRC32 names the
.I unix.elf
build whose symbols decode the dump; it is the value
.B "sysctl machdep.image_crc"
reported on the crashed kernel.
.PP
.I N
is read from
.IR directory /bounds
(0 when it does not exist), which is then advanced.
Both files are written under temporary names, synchronized, and renamed
into place, and the bounds file and the directory are synchronized before
.B savecore
clears the dump on the card, so a dump leaves the card only once a
complete copy is on stable storage.
A dump that fails a check, a copy that fails, or a directory without room
leaves the dump on the card and exits with status 1.
At securelevel 2 the kernel refuses every write open of the card, so
.B savecore
runs before the level rises.
.PP
.B savecore
copies a dump only when the file system holding
.I directory
keeps at least a reserve free after the dump and its info file are
written: 1024 kilobytes, or the number of kilobytes in
.IR directory /minfree .
.PP
The options are:
.TP
.B \-c
Clear the dump on the card without saving it.
.TP
.BI \-f " device"
Read the dump from
.I device
instead of the SD card's dump partition.
.TP
.B \-v
Report when no dump is present.
.SH EXAMPLES
A card image prepared on the build host with the tree's fsutil
.IP
fsutil \-\-repartition=fs=64m:dump=1m card.img
.PP
holds a file system on
.I sd0a
and a dump partition on
.IR sd0b .
After a panic and the reboot that follows it:
.IP
.nf
mount /dev/sd0a /mnt
mkdir \-p /mnt/crash
savecore /mnt/crash
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP 20
.IR directory /dump. N
the dump, sector for sector as the kernel wrote it
.TP
.IR directory /info. N
the dump's identity and segment table
.TP
.IR directory /bounds
the number of the next dump
.TP
.IR directory /minfree
the reserve in kilobytes
.SH SEE ALSO
reboot(8), sysctl(8)
.SH HISTORY
A
.B savecore
command appeared in 4.1BSD. This version follows the post-copy free space
rule and the clear-after-copy order of 4.4BSD's and copies no kernel file:
the RP2040 kernel executes from flash and is not a file on the root.
